{"kind":"artifact","text":"The application cache witness proves the migration this head actually makes, @12 to @13, instead of a migration nobody is performing. Exact clean pushed head f6489c78a4e9c11460ebe95e3312f1acd1d0de2c on request/decision-records-ui-recut; local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 340806ed, checkout clean.\n\nWHAT THIS PATH CHANGES, and it is one file. app_test.go, +49/-65. TestAnOlderProfileCacheIsRebuiltUnderTheNewRules is re-anchored to oldProfile @12, seeds a cached statement with Satisfier removed and its admitted value known, and asserts that replay under @13 restores that exact value and replaces the profile. The comments describe @12 to @13. The old @11 receipt-checkpoint scaffolding is deleted because the rule it pinned is preserved by the owning Workroom tests, which codex confirmed when it reviewed the same repair on the previous head.\n\nTHE DEFECT THIS REPLACES. The earlier version moved wantProfile to @13 but left oldProfile at @11 and the seeded observable at the @11 receipt-successor answer. A green run therefore said an @11 cache is rejected and said nothing whatever about @12 to @13 — and @12 is precisely the shape where statements carry no satisfier, which is what the replay must restore. A test that passes without exercising the transition under test is worse than no test, because it reports coverage that does not exist.\n\nTHE CONFLICT I RESOLVED HERE, stated because a reviewer should not have to reconstruct it. This file conflicted with main. The conflict was delete-versus-modify inside that one function: this head deletes the @11 scaffolding, while main had added AllowDeadBasis: true to two acts inside the block being deleted. I took the deletion — those additions die with the block and are referenced nowhere else — and then diffed the resulting function against the previous head's version to check nothing else moved. The only difference is main's own independent change, replacing the actRecord approval with a raw kernel.Submit, which main's new write-boundary guard requires because it refuses verdict-shaped generic state writes. So both sides' independent work survives, and the @12-to-@13 anchoring, seed and assertions are byte-identical to the reviewed head. Main's separate new test TestApprovedReportMustRestOnItsNamedArtifactBeforeSigning merged cleanly and is untouched.\n\nGATES RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0 with no output; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 33 packages, zero failures, zero cached; npm test 97/97; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"f6489c78a4e9c11460ebe95e3312f1acd1d0de2c","path":"internal/app"}}